ire:bytes:` `{session, all}` obligation in the same batch, with the session required on the exact bytes read and the obligation key required absent. The client Abort and the post-restore fence build that batch in one place (`abort::fence_batch`), so the two writers of the `Open → Aborting` edge cannot differ. The fence never moves the admission count: a fenced session still holds its `mpu:` record and its staged bytes, and stays counted until the terminal delete. A `Completing` session is refused `OperationAborted`, and a `Completed` or absent one answers `NoSuchUpload`, with nothing written. Two Aborts racing for one session answer one fenced and one already aborting when the store reports both commits' outcomes; an Abort whose own commit's outcome was unknown cannot tell its fence from a rival's byte-identical one, so it answers fenced too, which is harmless, since the session was fenced once, with one obligation. A lost commit is judged from what the store holds, never inferred: a conflict no re-read explains (on TiKV, a lock another writer held) is retried within a bounded number of rounds, and an obligation key found taken is damage only if the session is still unchanged when read again. An Abort that ends while a fence of it may still land, or before a read could say whether one landed, ends with that unknown outcome: an error that names the upload id and classifies as indeterminate, never one that says nothing was written. It has no production caller until the S3 verb is wired (#668). The custodian consumes both modes of obligation: its retirement leg (`wyrd_custodian::retirement`) deletes the records a `retire:records:` obligation names (#1061), and for a `retire:bytes:` one writes an `orphan:` mark naming the obligation at every fragment position it owes before, in a later batch, deleting the records that named them (#1062), in bounded batches either way — and, before it walks the ledger, walks each `Aborting` or `Completed` session's own `sidx:` range the same way — keeping how far it has got inside an entry that names more positions than one batch marks in a bookmark of its own, `custodian:owned-walk:<id>` (the entry's key and a count), removed with that entry or with the session, and where its next page starts, once it has passed entries it holds, in a scan position of its own, `custodian:owned-scan:<id>` (the key of the last entry passed), removed when the walk is back at the head of the range or with the session — and then deletes the session itself: its `mpu:` record, its `slot:` records and its `seggrp:` marker unless a `seg:` record names the nonce, in one batch conditioned on the session's exact bytes that lowers `mpuctl`'s count by one, the only writer that does (#1013; a `Completed` session waits for its tombstone window, an input the deployed custodian sets to keep it until `W_tombstone` is measured, #625) — and records how far it has got in an optional `cursor` field of the obligation, omitted until the drain first writes it, so no installer's bytes change, and every installer leaves room for it under the value ceiling (the runtime view's delete and reclamation section has the walk, and what holds an obligation). Nothing else writes or consumes them in production yet — the rest of the protocol (the gateway's fenced state transitions, staged publication) arrives with the later store round trips (#657–#658) and is specified in the proposal, not here. Because every transition it specifies is a compare-and-set on the record's exact bytes, the decoders enforce serialization identity now, before the protocol's writers exist to depend on it: the accepted set is exactly the encoder's image, byte for byte — an absent `content_type` is spelled only by omitting the field (the inode trio's omitted-when-absent rule), a noncanonical spelling of an accepted value (fields reordered, whitespace inserted) is a decode error rather than bytes a CAS could never match, and a part record's chunk values are read through a closed wire shape of their own, so an unknown or omitted chunk field is a decode error rather than a silent rewrite.
 
 `UploadPart`'s staging half gives `slot:` and `sidx:` their first writer: `wyrd_core::multipart::stage::stage_part` (#932). It reserves one in-flight slot, a free index of the session's fixed slot range, under `require_absent` on that key with the session required `Open` on its exact bytes, so the cap is the key space and a slot a crashed or refused attempt leaves counts toward it until the session is reclaimed. It then streams the body into chunks and registers each chunk's owned `sidx:` entry, with the placement its fragments will go to, before any of those fragments is written. That intent batch requires the session `Open` on the bytes the attempt was fenced on, and the absence of a drain record for every server it names (`desired:dserver:<id>`, a key that now lives in core and that the custodian shares), so it fails once the session has left `Open` or a selected server's drain is recorded; a chunk is planned around draining servers and re-planned when a drain lands first, never refused for it. Each intent checks every lease of the attempt at its own clock reading before it is sent, and refuses a lapsed one rather than reviving it. For as long as the request runs, including while a body read or a fragment write is pending (on a timer the caller supplies), one batch moves the slot's lease and every owned lease forward together, half a lease TTL after the last such batch (#933). It rewrites the slot in the same batch as every owned lease it renews, under the session fence, so a reaper fence that pinned the slot bytes it judged cannot land over a request that has since renewed. That renewal never holds staging past a pending write's deadline or the body-idle bound: work that answers while it commits is taken, and the renewal is then awaited to its end; one still committing when such a deadline passes is reported as an unknown outcome. Every round of a renewal checks every lease at its own clock reading, so a lapsed lease is refused, never revived. A renewal that fails, or finds the session gone from `Open`, stops staging at once; if a fragment write is then still pending, or the chunk store could not verify one, the error that stops staging is classed indeterminate, carries the renewal's cause and any doubt about its own commit, and is never a plain refusal. No renewal batch can exceed the per-batch operation budget `B_ops`: a knob set whose largest part would need one fails the knob clamps, and staging refuses a request whose chunk bound would. The staged attempt gives the part commit one last renewal of the same kind, which succeeds only when its leases still cover a span the caller names, judged at a clock reading taken after its last commit returned. Every fragment write carries an authorization deadline measured from the clock reading taken before its chunk's intent was committed, and staging waits for the writes no longer than that deadline: a write still pending then, or one the chunk store could not verify, is counted neither written nor not written, the error that stops staging is classed indeterminate in either case, and whatever such a write leaves sits at a position the chunk's owned entry already names. Every body read is bounded too: a read the client leaves without a non-empty piece for the caller's idle bound stops staging, and a piece or end of body that staging only sees once the bound has passed counts as none. An outcome the store could not report is settled by re-reading the batch's own record, never guessed, and an ambiguous reserve retries only the index it may already hold, so no attempt ever holds two slots. Staging writes no `part:` or `psum:` record and releases nothing: it hands its attempt, with one complete chunk reference per chunk, to the part commit (#927), and nothing calls it in production until the S3 `UploadPart` verb is wired (#669).
 
