    Work too large for the batch that creates it — the records a multipart publication leaves behind, a rolled-back completion attempt's segments — is recorded as a **retirement obligation**: one small record installed in that same batch, which the custodian drains afterwards, a bounded batch at a time, as part of every repair-loop pass. A pass looks at a fixed number of obligations, reads a fixed number of pages and commits a fixed number of batches, whatever the size of the ledger. It resumes after the last obligation the previous pass looked at and returns to the start once it reaches the end, keeping where to resume in one small record of its own, as GC's walk does; an obligation the drain cannot work on still counts against the pass, so a run of them delays the rest by at most a lap and never blocks them. Each batch deletes the next records the obligation names and writes the obligation back with its progress — a cursor kept inside the obligation itself, the batch conditioned on the obligation's exact stored bytes, so two drainers can never both advance it — and the batch that deletes the last record deletes the obligation with it. An obligation comes in one of two modes, named in its key. The first owes records whose bytes something else still protects — the published parts' records, whose bytes the published object now names, and a rolled-back attempt's segments, whose bytes the upload's own parts still name — and its drain only deletes, never writing a grace record. An attempt's segments are found by reading its range, since the obligation names the attempt and not how many segments it wrote, and they count as done only when the store says no segment follows the batch — a page the store returns shorter than asked is never taken for the end — and a read of the whole range from its start finds none left. An obligation the drain cannot act on — one that does not decode, one whose progress would no longer fit the store's value ceiling, one whose recorded progress disagrees with what the range holds, one whose batch the store refused — is left exactly as it is and named to the operator, and the drain goes on to the next. A pass reports nothing left to do only when it read the whole ledger, from its start to its end, and found no obligation in it; one that resumed part-way, or left an obligation unfinished, says so and the next pass goes on. Every obligation is installed with room left for its cursor under the value ceiling, so one written by this build always fits. The cursor is a field an older build does not know: downgrading past it loses no data, since GC protects by an obligation's presence and never decodes one, but the older post-restore pass reports an obligation carrying a cursor as unreadable.
 
-   The second mode owes **bytes** nothing will name once the obligation is gone — an aborted upload's parts and staged residue, the parts a completion left unnamed, a re-uploaded or losing part's chunks, a superseded or deleted generation's chunks or segments — and its drain **writes the grace record of every fragment position before it deletes the record that named it**, in an earlier commit, never the same one; a row whose bytes no record names goes with its last grace record. Each grace record names the obligation that wrote it, and GC leaves a fragment alone while that obligation is present, so the grace window of drained bytes **starts at the drain**: each batch is stamped with the repair loop's own clock, read for that batch once its obligation is in hand, never the pass's earlier reading, so an obligation installed while the pass ran gets grace records no older than itself. A record already naming this obligation is left as it is; one naming another event, or none, is replaced under a condition on its exact value; an absent one is written under a condition that it is still absent. A record GC has moved to *reclaiming* is never replaced, whatever it names: where no record the drain keeps names its position, GC destroys the fragment and deletes the record itself, and the drain writes a fresh one after, which GC's sweep of records with no fragment beneath them deletes once the obligation is gone and the late-write deadline has passed — as it does every grace record the drain wrote over a position with no fragment; where an upload part the drain still keeps names it, GC never moves (the upload's own protection outranks the record) and the drain does not either — the obligation is held, named to the operator as needing a human, and only an operator's repair moves it. A segmented generation's segments are read when the drain reaches them, so a fragment a repair moved before the supersede is marked where it now is. The drain checks every placement before it marks from it and holds an upload part whose placement is not one server per fragment, deleting nothing under it; it holds an obligation whose record it was part-way through marking is gone when it returns, since nothing is left to name the rest of that record's positions; it holds an aborted upload's obligation while the upload still owns staged residue, which a later walk empties; and it deletes no part of an upload whose rolled-back attempt still has segments owed, so the post-restore check never reads an honest half-drained state as damage. Each batch reads and writes at most a fixed number of grace records and records, whatever the size of the obligation.
+   The second mode owes **bytes** nothing will name once the obligation is gone — an aborted upload's parts and staged residue, the parts a completion left unnamed, a re-uploaded or losing part's chunks, a superseded or deleted generation's chunks or segments — and its drain **writes the grace record of every fragment position before it deletes the record that named it**, in an earlier commit, never the same one; a row whose bytes no record names goes with its last grace record. Each grace record names the obligation that wrote it, and GC leaves a fragment alone while that obligation is present, so the grace window of drained bytes **starts at the drain**: each batch is stamped with the repair loop's own clock, read for that batch once its obligation is in hand, never the pass's earlier reading, so an obligation installed while the pass ran gets grace records no older than itself. A record already naming this obligation is left as it is; one naming another event, or none, is replaced under a condition on its exact value; an absent one is written under a condition that it is still absent. A record GC has moved to *reclaiming* is never replaced, whatever it names: where no record the drain keeps names its position, GC destroys the fragment and deletes the record itself, and the drain writes a fresh one after, which GC's sweep of records with no fragment beneath them deletes once the obligation is gone and the late-write deadline has passed — as it does every grace record the drain wrote over a position with no fragment; where an upload part the drain still keeps names it, GC never moves (the upload's own protection outranks the record) and the drain does not either — the obligation is held, named to the operator as needing a human, and only an operator's repair moves it. A segmented generation's segments are read when the drain reaches them, so a fragment a repair moved before the supersede is marked where it now is. The drain checks every placement before it marks from it and holds an upload part whose placement is not one server per fragment, deleting nothing under it — unless the upload was aborted, when the part is retired by chunk id as described below; it holds an obligation whose record it was part-way through marking is gone when it returns, since nothing is left to name the rest of that record's positions; it waits on an ended upload's obligation while the upload still owns staged residue, which the upload's own teardown below empties first; and it deletes no part of an upload whose rolled-back attempt still has segments owed, so the post-restore check never reads an honest half-drained state as damage. Each batch reads and writes at most a fixed number of grace records and records, whatever the size of the obligation.
+
+   A multipart upload that has ended — aborted, or completed — **leaves the store at a terminal delete**, in the same repair-loop pass, before the obligations are walked. The custodian walks the upload's own staging entries — the residue of a part whose write stopped before it committed — writing the grace record of every position an entry names, under the upload's own identity, and deleting the entry in a later commit; nothing refills that range once the upload has ended, so the entries left are the progress. An entry can name more positions than one batch reads (a wide erasure scheme, or a damaged placement), so the walk keeps how far it has got inside one entry in one small record of the custodian's own per upload, written in the batch whose grace records it counts under a condition on its exact value, and removed with that entry or, at the latest, with the upload. It names the first entry the last batch left that the walk can still work on — whatever it covered of it — and the next batch walks that entry first, so an entry held for the operator ahead of it never stalls it. Entries held for the operator stay in the range, so a second small record per upload says where the walk's next read starts: a batch that marks and deletes nothing and holds something moves it past the held entries at the head of what it read, and once nothing is left after it the walk is back at the start of the range and the record is deleted. Every entry is therefore read once a lap however many are held ahead of it, and each held entry is named to the operator again every lap. That record is a position, never evidence — what an entry's deletion needs does not change with it, and one that names nothing of the upload's range is named to the operator and read as the start. It is written in a commit of its own, outside the pass's batch budget so that uploads with held entries cannot starve the rest, at most twice per upload a pass, put only while the upload's record is unchanged, and removed at the latest with the upload. Once a pass finds the range empty and no obligation of the upload left, one batch, conditioned on the upload's record exactly as read, deletes the record and the slots its parts reserved, deletes the reservation of its segment group unless a published object adopted the group, and lowers the admission count by one: of two drainers that race to it, one commits and the other's batch is refused, so the count goes down exactly once and an admission slot is released once. A **completed** upload's record is the tombstone that answers a retried completion with the published result, so it is kept, and its admission slot with it, until its tombstone window has passed; nothing measures that window yet, so the deployed custodian keeps every completed upload's record for now. For an **aborted** upload only, a record whose placement cannot be trusted — a part or a staging entry whose placement is not one server per fragment, or a staging entry that will not decode under a key that still names its chunk — is retired **by chunk id**: every fragment of that chunk that the D servers' own listings show gets a grace record, and for a staging entry that decodes so does every position it names, before the record goes. This runs only on a pass that sees every server the operator configured, the condition GC already runs under, since a listing that misses a server would leave that server's fragments with neither record nor grace record; on any other pass the record is held and named to the operator, and nothing is lost while it waits. The positions a record names itself are a fixed order, so the drain resumes inside them from its cursor across as many batches as they need. A listing is no such order — a fragment that drops out of it moves every later one — so a batch that reaches a chunk's listed fragments reads all of them, and progress is counted a whole chunk at a time; a chunk listed with more fragments than one batch reads is held and named to the operator as needing a human. A batch lists the fleet at most once, so a pass lists it at most once per batch it commits, plus once per aborted upload whose damaged record is held after its listing. One gap is stated rather than hidden: a write the aborted upload had authorized that lands after the listing, at a position the damaged record does not name, has neither record nor grace record, and GC keeps it — kept for good, never lost, never reclaimed without an operator. A part record that will not decode names no chunk in its key, so it is held for a human, and the upload stays until one acts. A grace record the walk wrote over a position whose write never landed stays as well, since that write may still land there.
 3. **Propagate across zones (L3).** If the object had replicas, the deletion propagates through the replica catalog: each holding zone drops its catalog record and its GC reclaims the local fragments. Until propagation completes, a *stale-tolerant* read in a lagging zone may still see the old object, bounded by replication lag (section 8.1); consistency-sensitive reads route to the home zone and never do.
 4. **Prove it (audit + crypto-erase).** The deletion is recorded in the append-only audit/event log — the operator's compliance and GDPR-deletion story (section 8.3). Where per-tenant envelope encryption is enabled, dropping the object/tenant key crypto-shreds the data immediately, making it unrecoverable independent of when GC runs (section 8.5).
